<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Gingindlovu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Gingindlovu was fought on 2 April 1879 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a>, when a British relief column under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a>, formed in an entrenched square, repulsed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> attack in coastal Zululand. The victory allowed the British to relieve the besieged garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshowe">Eshowe</a> the following day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gingindlovu.jpg" alt="Battle of Gingindlovu" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Illustrated London News · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_final_repulse_of_the_Zulus_at_Ginghilovo_-_ILN_1879.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Charles Pearson had led No. 1 Column across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thukela-river">Tugela River</a> and set up an advanced base at a deserted Norwegian mission station at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshowe">Eshowe</a>, where his men found themselves besieged. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a> organised a relief column of 3,390 Europeans and 2,280 Africans: sailors from HMS Shah and Boadicea, the 57th Regiment of Foot, the 91st Highlanders, the 60th Rifles, the Natal Native Contingent and mounted troops under Major Barrow, with two 9-pounder guns, four rocket tubes and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling guns</a>. It set out from Fort Tenedos on 29 March, travelling without baggage or tents.</p><p>Fearing a repeat of Isandlwana, Chelmsford took a route further east than Pearson's and entrenched his camp each evening. On 1 April he laagered on a ridge on the south bank of the Neyzane, behind a trench and a waist-high earth wall enclosing 120 wagons in a square. That night John Dunn, accompanied by Captain William Molyneux, swam the river and found the camp of an impi of 12,000 warriors. Prince Dabulamanzi kaMpande, half-brother of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>, persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> commanders to wait until morning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At dawn on 2 April, in thick mist, the impi crossed the river and attacked three sides of the laager in its chest and horns formation. The British infantry opened fire at 300 to 400 yards, supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling guns</a> and rockets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> marksmen caused a few casualties, but the charges lacked the drive seen at Isandlwana and Rorke's Drift, and the only Zulu to reach the laager was a 10-year-old boy, taken prisoner by the naval brigade. Around 7:00 a.m. Chelmsford sent out his mounted troops and the Natal Native Contingent, and many Zulu were killed as they retreated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Around the laager 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> bodies were counted, and 300 more Zulu were killed in the pursuit; the British also killed the Zulu wounded. British losses were two officers and nine men killed, among them a lieutenant-colonel, and four officers and fifty men wounded. The battle restored Chelmsford's confidence in his army, and with the last resistance removed he was able to advance and raise the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshowe">Eshowe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Gingindlovu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defeats at Rorke’s Drift, Kambula and <b>Gingindlovu</b> followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
